Hypothesis 1: Smart Discovery (Filters)

I: Let’s start with our first topic. Picture a club website that lets you filter clubs by interest, time commitment, and difficulty level to find matched results instantly. If you used these three filters right now, could you walk through your whole search process step by step?
S: Sure. I would start by selecting my personal interests first to narrow down the overall club categories and cut out anything I have zero passion for. After locking in my preferred interests, I would adjust the time commitment filter based on my weekly class schedule, study deadlines and personal downtime. I would rule out clubs that hold frequent long meetings or require extra offline work on weekdays. Next, I would pick a suitable difficulty range, mostly beginner or casual level, since I don’t have professional experience and prefer low-pressure environments. Once all three filters are applied, I will browse the refined club list, preview brief previews, and click into individual pages to compare details before I make a shortlist.

I: Among interest, time commitment and difficulty level, which one is the most important to you? Please explain your priority in detail.
S: I would rank personal interest as my top priority. For me, joining a club is a way to relax and enrich my campus life, not an extra mandatory task. Even if a club has flexible time and low difficulty, I will not stay engaged long-term if the theme and activities do not align with my hobbies. Time and difficulty are important secondary considerations, but they are flexible. I am willing to slightly adjust my schedule or learn basic new skills if I truly enjoy a club’s focus.

I: What website features or small design details would make this filtering process feel effortless and user-friendly?
S: I hope the filter bar is placed in a fixed, visible position on the homepage, with one-click selection and real-time result updates without reloading the whole page. It would also be helpful to have clear classification labels, adjustable slider options for time hours, and simple icon markers for difficulty levels. In addition, a one-click reset button and saved filter history would make repeated searches much easier and save me extra operation time.

I: Are there still moments during this search where you would feel unsure or confused, even with these three main filters? Why would that happen?
S: Yes, I would still feel uncertain sometimes. The three basic filters only show superficial labels, but many clubs have hidden workload or mixed activity styles. For example, a club labeled “low time commitment” might still require voluntary weekend events or group assignments outside regular meetings. Also, some clubs mark themselves as beginner-friendly but contain hidden skill requirements, so I cannot fully judge the actual experience only by filter tags.

I: Besides these three core filters, what other key information do you need to view to feel confident a club is a good fit for you?
S: After filtering, I need to see real activity frequency, member feedback, general group size, and whether the club charges extra fees. I also want to know if new members receive guidance and whether the activity content is stable every semester. These practical details help me avoid clubs that look good on the filter but fail to meet my daily needs.

I: Are there any extra custom filters you wish this website added to help you find your ideal club faster?
S: I would love additional filters such as membership fee, club atmosphere type (casual vs competitive), member scale, new member friendliness, and activity format, like offline gathering, online sharing or outdoor events. These targeted filters could help me exclude unsuitable options more accurately and shorten my selection time.
Hypothesis 2: Social-driven Features

I: Moving to social-driven features. Imagine this website clearly shows when your friends have joined or marked interest in a certain club. How much would this influence you to click in and explore that club?
S: It would have a very strong influence on my exploration behaviour. I usually only search for familiar, mainstream clubs on my own and rarely take the initiative to discover niche groups. If I notice my friends are interested or already joined a club, I will naturally develop curiosity. I will actively check its activity content, atmosphere and joining requirements, even if it is a field I have never considered before.

I: Would you actively choose to join a club specifically because your friends are already there? Why or why not?
S: I would not join a club only for my friends, but their participation would greatly increase my willingness to apply. Entering a new group alone can be socially intimidating, and having familiar friends inside helps me adapt faster, reduce awkwardness during group activities, and make participation more enjoyable. That said, I still require the club’s overall content and style to match my preferences, and I will never force myself to join a completely unsuitable club just for social reasons.

I: What kind of friend-related social features would make you want to log in and use this club website more regularly?
S: I would enjoy a simple friend activity feed that shows newly joined clubs, upcoming events friends plan to attend, and short reviews they leave. A lightweight follow system and club sharing function would also encourage daily use. When I can easily see what my peers are experiencing and share my own discoveries, the platform becomes more interactive instead of a one-time search tool.

I: Do you think viewing your friends’ club activity would push you to try unique clubs you would never join on your own?
S: Absolutely. I tend to stay inside my comfort zone and stick to similar hobby choices all the time. Peer social influence can break this fixed preference. Seeing friends engage in creative, artistic, volunteer or sports clubs I normally ignore would motivate me to try new experiences, expand my hobbies and make my campus life more diverse.

I: What other creative social functions do you want on a club platform to make joining and participating more fun?
S: I hope to have a public Q&A section for current members, a club event sharing wall, and a simple group poll function for upcoming activities. It would also be fun to have a member highlight section and a beginner buddy system that pairs new students with senior members. These social designs build a stronger community vibe and make club participation more interactive and interesting.
Hypothesis 3: Clear Club Information

I: Next is clear club information. In this ideal scenario, every club profile displays regular activities, fixed meeting times, entry requirements and real member daily experience. What is the single most important detail you need to see before applying?
S: The most critical information for me is the exact time arrangement and mandatory participation rules. I need to know how often meetings are held, how long each activity lasts, and whether members are required to join every event. Clear time expectations help me balance my study, rest and extracurricular time, and avoid conflicts that force me to quit halfway.

I: Do you believe the current overall structure of online club information, including activities, time and rules, is clear and complete? If not, what is missing or confusing?
S: I think current club information is generally vague and overly promotional. Most introductions only display attractive highlights but ignore practical, necessary details. They often lack specific weekly plans, threshold limits for new members, hidden extra costs, internal management styles, and the real daily atmosphere. Many descriptions are too general, leaving new students confused about what the club actually does.

I: How would well-organized, transparent club information help you make a faster and more accurate joining decision?
S: Clear and structured information allows me to compare multiple clubs side by side efficiently. I can quickly filter out options that conflict with my schedule, hobbies or expectations without repeatedly messaging club leaders to ask basic questions. When all key details are displayed directly on the profile, my doubt and hesitation decrease, helping me finish evaluation and final decisions in a much shorter time.

I: Is there any specific detail about club culture and community atmosphere that you think should be permanently included on every club page?
S: I hope every profile includes an honest introduction about group culture, such as whether the environment is relaxed and low-pressure, how leaders communicate with new members, and how active regular members are. It is also important to mention if the club welcomes introverted students, freshmen with no experience, and whether the overall tone is casual, academic or performance-oriented.

I: What type of comprehensive profile information would make you 100% confident that this club is perfectly suitable for you?
S: I would feel fully confident with detailed monthly activity plans, clear joining requirements, real photos and short clips from past events, honest member testimonials, transparent fee notes, and a detailed introduction of long-term club goals. When both advantages and potential challenges are presented objectively, I can judge whether I fit the community rhythm and activity style completely.
Hypothesis 4: Easy Joining Process

I: Lastly, talk about the joining process. This website provides a simplified step-by-step online application, and users can finish the whole sign-up within only a few minutes. How would this simplicity change your motivation to join clubs?
S: A fast, paperless online joining process would significantly boost my overall motivation to participate. Traditional club registration often involves complicated forms, offline submission and slow review, which makes me feel lazy and give up easily. If I can complete application quickly online with low effort, I am more willing to explore multiple clubs, try new things and overcome the psychological barrier of signing up.

I: In this ideal easy joining system, what is the minimum number of steps and basic personal information you think is necessary?
S: I think 3 to 4 simple steps are reasonable and efficient. The required information should only include basic student identity details, active contact methods, available weekly time slots, and a short simple self-introduction. Long written essays, redundant personal background questions and repeated data filling should all be removed to keep the process concise.

I: What traditional joining procedures, such as physical paperwork or long waiting periods, do you want to eliminate completely?
S: I hope to fully cancel offline paper registration forms, unnecessary in-person interviews for ordinary clubs, overly complicated admission essays, and long manual approval waiting times. Many traditional formalities are repetitive and unnecessary for student clubs; they only waste time and reduce students’ willingness to join.

I: Even if the joining steps are extremely simple and streamlined, what factors might still stop you from finishing your application?
S: Unclear club positioning and ambiguous activity planning would still stop me. If the club profile is vague, the joining rules are unreasonable, or member expectations are too demanding, I will hesitate and abandon the application. Simple operation cannot make up for confusing content, and I will not join a group with unstable or unclear development direction.

I: If you could fully redesign it, how would you create a perfect, quick, and enjoyable joining experience for all students?
S: I would design a clean one-page application interface with a clear progress bar to show current steps. After submission, users receive instant confirmation and clear reminders about follow-up interview results or group arrangements. The whole design should be intuitive, with no pop-up ads or redundant steps, and offer simple modification options if students need to edit their answers before final submission.
Interview Closing

I: That concludes all our planned interview questions today. Do you have any additional comments, general suggestions, or personal expectations for this club discovery website?
S: Overall, I hope the platform focuses on practicality and clarity. It should balance useful filtering tools, honest club information, friendly social interaction and a smooth joining process. It does not need overcomplicated decorative features; instead, it should prioritize solving students’ real pain points when searching and joining clubs. A simple, reliable and transparent system will be the most popular for long-term use.

I: Thank you so much for your thoughtful, detailed and honest answers throughout this interview. Your feedback provides valuable reference for our website design and user research. I truly appreciate your time and cooperation.
S: You’re very welcome. It was a great experience sharing my thoughts, and I hope my opinions can help with your project.